Frequently Asked Questions
What is a stock market API and what can developers use it for?
A stock market API is a web service that delivers financial data (such as real-time prices, historical OHLCV data, earnings reports, and fundamentals) directly to your application. Developers use them to build trading bots, portfolio trackers, financial dashboards, and backtesting engines without scraping exchanges directly.
Are there free stock market APIs, and what are their limitations?
Most providers offer a free tier, but with significant constraints. Alpha Vantage caps free usage at 25 requests per day, EODHD at 20 requests per day, and Financial Modeling Prep at 250 requests per day. Real-time data and full historical depth are almost always locked behind paid plans, so free tiers are best suited for prototyping rather than production use.
When should I use a real-time stock API versus a historical data API?
Use a real-time API (typically via WebSocket) when building live trading systems, price alert tools, or streaming dashboards where millisecond latency matters. Historical (REST-based) APIs are better suited for backtesting strategies, building valuation models, or generating charts, and are generally cheaper to access.
Which stock API is best for backtesting trading strategies?
Financial Modeling Prep and EODHD are strong choices for backtesting: both provide 30+ years of historical price data across a wide range of tickers and exchanges. Alpha Vantage also covers long historical windows and adds technical indicators. If you need deep derivatives history, Intrinio offers up to 50 years of data but at a higher price point starting around $3,000 per year.
Can stock market APIs be used with AI agents and MCP?
Yes. Several providers now support the Model Context Protocol (MCP), which lets AI agents discover and call financial data endpoints autonomously. Alpha Vantage ships a native MCP server, and other APIs can be registered as MCP tools using JSON schema definitions, enabling use cases like AI-driven market analysis and conversational financial research.
What is the difference between a stock market API and a currency or forex API?
Stock market APIs focus on equities, ETFs, options, and fundamentals, while currency APIs (like Abstract's Exchange Rates API) specialize in real-time and historical forex and cryptocurrency rates across 150+ currency pairs. For fintech apps that need both equity prices and currency conversion, it is common to combine a dedicated stock API with a separate exchange rates API rather than relying on a single provider for everything.
